# The orchestrator loop: the runner moves work between waves, critics stop it when judgment is needed, a person answers the stop. Owner decision 2026-09-13. 2026-09-13
#
# DISPATCH: ./scripts/dispatch.sh git@github.com:Arlencho/dev-agents.git wave-plans/dev-agents/2026-09-13-orchestrator-loop.plan --detach --retries 1
# Why: on 2026-09-13 every hop between waves waited for the orchestrator to notice; the judgment the orchestrator added lived in scope calls and cheap-path calls, never in the hop itself. Automate the relay, make the judgment points loud.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1 | devops | Branch feat/orchestrator-loop from origin/main. Extend scripts/queue-runner.sh and scripts/dispatch.sh, keeping every existing behaviour and test green. ONE, memory guard: before any start the runner reads free memory and swap the way docs/memory notes describe (vm_stat and sysctl vm.swapusage on macOS); when free memory is under a threshold or swap used is over a threshold, both in config with defaults of 50 percent free and 3.5 GB swap, it starts nothing, logs the reason once per change of state, and writes it into the queue render so the Floor shows why nothing starts; resuming is automatic when the numbers recover, and nothing is ever killed by the runner. TWO, an AFTER header: a plan may carry the line AFTER: <plan path>; the runner treats such a plan as blocked until the named plan has a dispatch_end with outcome landed or success in logs/dispatch-runs, and shows the reason in place. THREE, one automatic fix round: when a dispatch ends with a critic seat whose posted verdict first line carries BLOCK-FIX (reuse the verdict parsing in scripts/desk_live.py, do not write a second parser), the runner writes a fix plan next to the original named <plan>-fix1.plan with one producer seat of the same role and branch whose task is the critic comment quoted in full plus the words fix every finding and add a test per finding, followed by the same critic seat for round 2, and queues it with AFTER the original; a second BLOCK-FIX on the fix plan queues nothing and the item stays a stop; BLOCK-ESCALATE, BLOCK-CLOSE and any unparsed verdict queue nothing. FOUR, landing: when every critic seat of a dispatch posted SAFE-TO-MERGE or APPROVE-MERGE and the PR checks are green and the merge state is clean, the runner calls scripts/land.sh for that PR, which already merges with read-back and sweeps worktrees; a draft PR is marked ready first; anything else is a stop, never a merge. FIVE, every stop the runner produces (guard active, second block, escalate, red checks, merge refused) is one line in a stops file the desk reads, so the Floor NEEDS YOU section shows it with the critic sentence and one action; no new page work here, only the data. Tests for each of the five with fixtures (a fake vm_stat, a plan with AFTER, a fixture dispatch with a BLOCK-FIX comment, a fixture with all SAFE and green, a red check). Update docs for the runner and the plan header format. VERIFY: the runner test suite and make queue-runner-install --dry-run or its equivalent pass with real exit codes in the PR body; run the runner once by hand with --dry-run against the live queue and paste the output. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Open a draft PR titled The orchestrator loop: runner guards, AFTER, one fix round, landing. | feat/orchestrator-loop

2 | devops | On branch feat/orchestrator-loop after wave 1: make the verdict vocabulary the rule in every critic role under roles (backend-critic, frontend-critic, api-critic, database-critic, plan-critic, devops-critic, security-reviewer) and in the shared critic skill if one exists under skills. The rule in plain words: BLOCK-FIX means the producer can fix it without a decision by anyone, and the runner will fire one fix round automatically; BLOCK-ESCALATE means a person must decide, and the critic must name why in the first line after the verdict, from this list: scope grew, PRD is wrong or silent, pre-existing defect found, cheaper path exists, security judgment; BLOCK-CLOSE means the PR should not land at all; SAFE-TO-MERGE means the runner may land it. A critic that finds both a fixable defect and a judgment case posts BLOCK-ESCALATE, never BLOCK-FIX. Add the same rule to CLAUDE.md of dev-agents and to docs where the critic convention is documented, and add a test that every critic role file contains the rule block verbatim. VERIFY: the role tests pass with real exit codes. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Commit, push, update the PR body. | feat/orchestrator-loop

3 | plan-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the PR on branch feat/orchestrator-loop. No commits, no push, no review, no labels; tmp writes only. Record head SHA at start and at report. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: can the runner ever do something a person would have stopped. Attack it: a BLOCK-FIX whose comment also contains an escalation sentence must not fire a fix round; a fix round must never fire twice; a merge must never happen on a draft with a red check, on a PR with one critic silent, on a PR whose checks predate the head SHA, or on a repo whose branch protection refuses; the memory guard must never kill and must resume; AFTER on a plan whose parent failed must stay blocked and say so; the stops file must never carry a prompt, a path or a secret. Write failing tests where the PR falls short and cite them. Post ONE comment on the PR whose first line reads CRITIC ORCHESTRATOR LOOP with SAFE-TO-MERGE, BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE and citations. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. | feat/orchestrator-loop
